  "transcript": "# Machinery of government changes\n\n## Was abolishing DSIT the right move?\n> Most witnesses thought getting rid of DSIT was a mistake because it fragmented science, technology and innovation policy, though a few welcomed locating AI closer to the centre.\n\nChair: We opened by asking whether DSIT had been effective and whether its abolition was justified.\nAlex Thomas: The Institute for Government did not campaign for DSIT to be abolished; on balance we think getting rid of it was a mistake—machinery changes are disruptive and should be very well justified.\nDr Simon Kaye: DSIT had built relationships with the science and tech community and was becoming effective; I worry about the opportunity costs of constant reshuffles but recognise the Government may have reasons for shifting AI closer to No. 10.\nGeorge Freeman: As a former Minister, I said DSIT felt at times like an 'AI Department' and questioned whether wet labs and regional science clusters were getting enough attention.\nSir Paul Nurse: I called the abolition a big mistake: science should be central across Government, not squeezed into a general business department.\n\n\n## Disruption, costs and civil‑service morale\n> Machinery-of-government moves cost money, distract staff, hurt morale and take years to show benefits; civil servants often bear the hidden costs.\n\nDaniel Zeichner: How have civil servants reacted—are they pleased or fed up?\nAlex Thomas: Most are fed up. Reorganisations are disruptive behind the scenes: IT, HR, turf battles. We estimated a typical MoG change cost about £15m in 2019—roughly £20m today—plus opportunity costs.\nDr Simon Kaye: People value continuity. If personnel and relationships stay stable, structures can be made to work; but constantly shifting 'deckchairs' damages momentum.\nDr Laura Gilbert: From delivery teams' experience, sudden moves make staff feel fungible; top talent leaves and rebuilding technical capability takes months to years.\n\n\n## Who owns AI policy and regulation?\n> AI responsibilities are scattered across Departments; witnesses urged a clear home or a strong central forcing mechanism with technical and analytical capacity.\n\nChair: AI policy is now split—BIST, Cabinet Office, DCMS—with no clear place for regulation. Where should regulation sit?\nDr Simon Kaye: The Government seems to be balancing opportunity and risk by pushing AI closer to the centre and national security; the AI Security Institute was intended to be central to this.\nAlex Thomas: Fragmentation risks confusion. The important thing is clarity: let AI sit somewhere coherent, or have a forcing mechanism from the centre—Cabinet committee or taskforce—that can make binding choices.\nTom Collins: You suggested a forcing mechanism; what would that look like in practice?\nAlex Thomas: A Cabinet committee or a properly resourced taskforce with authority from No.10, not just a grouping of interested Ministers, so decisions aren't left unresolved between departments.\nDr Laura Gilbert: If there is a taskforce, it must include technical and analytical capability—people who build and evaluate systems—so it can both regulate and advise on adoption properly.\nDom Hallas: The taskforce should stay sharp and agile: focus on a few critical, fast-moving issues and be resourced to act quickly, rather than trying to be a new ministry in disguise.\nChair: Members stressed the Committee's role in pulling threads across departments if Whitehall becomes more diffuse.\n\n\n## Research funding, UKRI and the STFC crisis\n> Witnesses warned that moving research policy into a large business department risks sidelining basic research; STFC facility cuts and budget erosion from inflation need transparent explanations.\n\nFreddie van Mierlo: STFC cuts threaten major national facilities—should the Government revisit those decisions?\nDr Alicia Greated: CaSE members fear narrowing of the research base. Facilities have fixed costs and repeated cuts risk losing people and even operations; decisions must be transparent.\nSir Paul Nurse: Science is an ecosystem: discovery (curiosity‑driven), translation and application all matter. We have to protect discovery research even when focusing on economic growth.\nKit Malthouse: Is it realistic to expect a busy Business Secretary to prioritise science or will it be drowned by other political fires?\nSir Paul Nurse: Good people and political friends in Treasury and No.10 help; structures matter less than whether leaders treat science as a priority and protect long‑term budgets.\nGeorge Freeman: Treasury control of budgets increases after reshuffles; we need better models for funding infrastructure—capital plus sustainable revenue—and public/private approaches.\nDr Alicia Greated: We need a clear international R&D strategy, oversight of R&D capability across the UK, and long-term stability so universities and institutes can plan.\n\n\n## Delivery, procurement and regional innovation\n> To turn science into commercial success, Government must fix procurement, support startups, strengthen local clusters, and evaluate structural changes rather than reshuffle by impulse.\n\nMartin Wrigley: How can Government be 'Britain's best customer' so start-ups win anchor contracts?\nDom Hallas: Procurement culture favours big incumbents. Fixes: better pre‑market engagement, easier direct awards for small contracts, and social‑value rules calibrated so they don't punish tech productivity.\nDr Laura Gilbert: Programs like the Sovereign AI Fund and NSSIF can direct early investment and link it to procurement, but procurement rules must be reworked and evaluated for real outcomes.\nMaya Ellis: What about regional clusters and devolution—how will the changes affect local innovation in places without big mayors?\nDom Hallas: Bundling research funding and business finance in BIST could help bridge grant-to-private finance gaps outside London, but delivery will depend on joined-up action with local bodies.\nDr Laura Gilbert: Digital transformation needs shared learning and open‑source delivery so local authorities can reuse systems rather than each reinventing the wheel.\nAlex Thomas: One practical recommendation: independently evaluate machinery‑of‑government changes—costs, benefits, disruption—so future decisions are evidence‑based, not instinctive.\nChair: Committee members pressed for clarity on where digital infrastructure and GDS now sit and for ensuring political attention and funding follow strategic priorities.\n",
